#: ../../source/algorithms/lyapunov.rst:4
msgid "Lyapunov exponent computation"
msgstr "Cálculo de expoentes de Lyapunov"

#: ../../source/algorithms/lyapunov.rst:7
msgid "Motivation"
msgstr "Motivação"

#: ../../source/algorithms/lyapunov.rst:9
msgid ""
"In chaotic dynamics, the **largest Lyapunov exponent** "
":math:`\\lambda_{\\max}` quantifies how quickly two initially nearby "
"trajectories diverge from each other.  If :math:`\\lambda_{\\max} > 0`, "
"the system is chaotic: a tiny perturbation in the initial condition grows"
" exponentially and the long-term behaviour becomes unpredictable.  If "
":math:`\\lambda_{\\max} \\le 0`, the system is periodic or stable."
msgstr ""
"In chaotic dynamics, the **largest Lyapunov exponent** "
":math:`\\lambda_{\\max}` quantifies how quickly two initially nearby "
"trajectories diverge from each other.  If :math:`\\lambda_{\\max} > "
"0`, the system is chaotic — a tiny perturbation in the initial condition "
"grows exponentially and the long-term behaviour becomes unpredictable.  "
"If :math:`\\lambda_{\\max} \\le 0`, the system is periodic or "
"stable."

#: ../../source/algorithms/lyapunov.rst:16
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"For chaotic communication, :math:`\\lambda_{\\max}` is the single most "
"important diagnostic: it tells us whether the Hénon map with a given FIR "
"filter still preserves chaos, or whether the filter *killed* the chaos "
"(making the scheme useless for physical-layer security)."
msgstr ""
"For chaotic communication, :math:`\\lambda_{\\max}` is the single "
"most important diagnostic: it tells us whether the Hénon map with a given"
" FIR filter still preserves chaos, or whether the filter *killed* the "
"chaos (making the scheme useless for physical-layer security)."

#: ../../source/algorithms/lyapunov.rst:22
msgid "Mathematical definition"
msgstr "Definição matemática"

#: ../../source/algorithms/lyapunov.rst:24
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"For a discrete-time dynamical system :math:`\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = "
"f(\\mathbf{x}_n)`, the largest Lyapunov exponent is defined as the time-"
"averaged exponential divergence rate:"
msgstr ""
"For a discrete-time dynamical system :math:`\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = "
"f(\\mathbf{x}_n)`, the largest Lyapunov exponent is defined as the "
"time-averaged exponential divergence rate:"

#: ../../source/algorithms/lyapunov.rst:28
msgid ""
"\\lambda_{\\max}\n"
"= \\lim_{N \\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{N}\n"
"  \\sum_{k=1}^{N} \\ln \\frac{\\| \\delta \\mathbf{x}_k \\|}\n"
"                            {\\| \\delta \\mathbf{x}_{k-1} \\|},"
msgstr ""
"\\lambda_{\\max}\\n= \\lim_{N \\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{N}\\n  "
"\\sum_{k=1}^{N} \\ln \\frac{\\| \\delta \\mathbf{x}_k "
"\\|}\\n                            {\\| \\delta \\mathbf{x}_{k-1}"
" \\|},"

#: ../../source/algorithms/lyapunov.rst:35
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"where :math:`\\delta \\mathbf{x}_k` is the evolved tangent vector at step"
" :math:`k`, obtained by applying the Jacobian :math:`J(\\mathbf{x}_k)` "
"and re-normalising."
msgstr ""
"where :math:`\\delta \\mathbf{x}_k` is the evolved tangent vector at "
"step :math:`k`, obtained by applying the Jacobian "
":math:`J(\\mathbf{x}_k)` and re-normalising."

#: ../../source/algorithms/lyapunov.rst:39
msgid ""
"In the code, this is the **\"Gram-Schmidt\"** (more precisely, **Modified"
" Gram-Schmidt  (MGS)**) approach to Lyapunov spectra.  The MGS variant is"
" more stable numerically than classical Gram-Schmidt, especially when the"
" tangent vectors become nearly parallel after many iterations."
msgstr ""
"In the code, this is the **\\** (more precisely, **Modified Gram-Schmidt "
"— MGS**) approach to Lyapunov spectra.  The MGS variant is more stable "
"numerically than classical Gram-Schmidt, especially when the tangent "
"vectors become nearly parallel after many iterations."

